# Loan Default Prediction

A machine learning project predicting whether a mortgage loan applicant will default, using the [Kaggle Loan Default Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/yasserh/loan-default-dataset) (~148,670 loans, 34 original features).

## Problem statement

Lenders need to assess default risk at the point of loan application, using only information available *before* the loan's outcome is known. This project builds and compares three classification models, with a strong emphasis on avoiding data leakage and understanding *why* the models make the predictions they do.

## Dataset

- 148,670 rows, 34 original columns
- - Binary target: `Status` (0 = no default, 1 = default), ~75% / 25% class split
  - - Mix of borrower financials, loan structure, property/collateral details, and credit history
   
    - ## Approach
   
    - ### 1. Leakage detection
    - Three columns (`rate_of_interest`, `Interest_rate_spread`, `Upfront_charges`) were missing almost exclusively for defaulted loans, a sign they're only populated *after* loan origination, and therefore not legitimately available at prediction time. These were dropped.
   
    - ### 2. Missingness as a feature
    - Other columns with missing values (`property_value`, `LTV`, `dtir1`, `income`) were plausibly missing due to incomplete applications, information that *would* be available at decision time. Rather than discarding this signal, binary `_missing` flags were engineered before median imputation.
   
    - ### 3. Encoding & preprocessing
    - - Ordinal encoding for `age` (preserving the natural bracket order)
      - - One-hot encoding (with `drop_first=True`) for 20 nominal categorical columns
        - - Feature scaling (`StandardScaler`) for the linear model and neural network
         
          - ### 4. Models trained
          - | Model | Notes |
          - |---|---|
          - | Logistic Regression | Baseline + `class_weight='balanced'` + threshold tuning sweep |
          - | Random Forest | 100 trees, `class_weight='balanced'` |
          - | Neural Network (Keras) | 2 hidden layers (64/32 units), dropout, `class_weight` |
         
          - ### 5. Interpretability
          - SHAP (`TreeExplainer`) applied to the Random Forest model to identify and visualize feature importance and interaction effects.
         
          - ## Results
         
          - | Metric | Logistic Regression (threshold 0.35) | Random Forest | Neural Network |
          - |---|---|---|---|
          - | Accuracy | 86.3% | **89.1%** | 86.2% |
          - | Precision | 83.0% | **94.9%** | 71.3% |
          - | Recall | 56.0% | 59.0% | **73.4%** |
          - | F1 | 66.9% | 72.7% | 72.4% |
          - | ROC-AUC | 0.745 | 0.790 | **0.819** |
         
          - **No single model dominates.** Random Forest offers the highest precision (fewest false alarms), while the neural network achieves the best recall and overall ranking ability (ROC-AUC). In a real lending context, the right choice depends on the relative cost of a missed defaulter versus a false alarm.
         
          - ## Key findings
         
          - - **Data-missingness flags were the strongest predictors overall** (confirmed via SHAP), more informative than most raw feature values, and legitimately available at application time (unlike the leaked columns that were dropped).
            - - **`Credit_Score` showed surprisingly little predictive power**, contradicting the intuitive assumption that it would dominate.
              - - **`income` has a non-linear, diminishing-returns relationship** with default risk, strongly protective at low income levels, flattening out above roughly $6-7k.
               
